 "drivingQuestion": "From where do we know the Megilla is read by day?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "No refutation: the mishna addresses only the daytime reading",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "megillah.20a.8",
   "daf": "20a",
   "sentenceId": 7,
   "he": "גְּמָ׳ מְנָלַן? דְּאָמַר קְרָא: ״וְהַיָּמִים הָאֵלֶּה נִזְכָּרִים וְנַעֲשִׂים״ — בַּיּוֹם אִין, בַּלַּיְלָה לָא. לֵימָא תֶּיהְוֵי תְּיוּבְתָּא דְּרַבִּי יְהוֹשֻׁעַ בֶּן לֵוִי, דְּאָמַר רַבִּי יְהוֹשֻׁעַ בֶּן לֵוִי: חַיָּיב אָדָם לִקְרוֹת אֶת הַמְּגִילָּה בַּלַּיְלָה וְלִשְׁנוֹתָהּ בַּיּוֹם! כִּי קָתָנֵי — אַדְּיוֹם.",
   "en": "«GEMARA:» The Gemara asks: «From where do we» derive the halakha taught in the mishna that the Megilla may be read only during the day? The Gemara answers: «As the verse states: “And that these days should be remembered and kept”» (Esther 9:28). The word “days” indicates «during the day, yes,» but «at night, no.» The Gemara asks: «Let us say that this is a conclusive refutation of» the opinion of «Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi, as Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi said: A person is obligated to read the Megilla at night and» then «repeat it during the day.» The Gemara rejects this: There is no proof from here, «as when» the mishna «teaches» that the Megilla may be read only during the day, it was referring to «the daytime» reading, but the nighttime reading is not considered here at all."
  }
 ],
